    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Quatre Sept is played in the French-speaking provinces of Canada. It descends from the Italian game Tressette, which was fashionable throughout Europe in the 18th century. French players adapted Tressette to the 32-card pack (no 3 or 2), substituting 10 and 9 for those ranks. The game was exported to Canada with the ranking 10-9-A-K-Q-J-8-7.',
      originCountries: [
        'Canada',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '18th century: Tressette fashionable in Europe; 18th-century French/German sources mention three- and four-sevens bonuses.',
        "19th century: Tressette's sevens bonuses abandoned in Europe but survived in the Canadian variant.",
        'French adaptation: 32-card pack adopted; ranking 10-9-A-K-Q-J-8-7; Trois Sept â†’ Quatre Sept.',
        'Canadian development: Four sevens in hand = instant game win; La Politaine (Napolitane) bonus retained.',
        'Present: Played in Quebec and other French-Canadian regions.',
      ],
      evolution: 'Tressette (three sevens) became Trois Sept in France; the Canadian variant added the four-sevens instant win and was named Quatre Sept. La Politaine (from Italian Napolitaneâ€”10-9-A of a suit) gives an 11-point bonus when used to win three consecutive tricks.',
      cultural: 'A staple of French-Canadian card play. The alternative name La Poule may derive from La Politaine. Related German variant Pollack (19th century) uses similar scoring with declaration bonuses.',
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: '4 players in fixed partnerships. Partners sit opposite each other.',
      deck: '32-card French pack: 10, 9, A, K, Q, J, 8, 7 in each suit. No trumps.',
      equipment: '32-card deck, score sheet.',
      dealing: '1. Deal 8 cards each in batches of 3-2-3. 2. Deal and play clockwise. 3. If any player has all four sevens, that team wins the game immediately.',
    },
